  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Afro-Brazilian religion of São Luís, Maranhão, centred on the Casa das Minas, founded in the nineteenth century by Dahomean (Jeje) women and devoted to voduns, and on the Casa de Nagô, with many later houses across Maranhão and Pará. Initiated women sing and dance to songs in Jeje- and Nagô-derived liturgical languages, accompanied by drums, an iron bell (ferro or gã) and gourd rattles (cabaças). A generic membrane-drum card stands in for the drums, the source-unspecified agogô for the iron and xequerê for the cabaças.",
